\section{Correction of One Judge}
\label{app:judge}

The labels of the Gemma-2-27B judge in an earlier version of this paper came from an incorrect forward pass: the default attention implementation of the transformers library (sdpa) omits the attention soft-capping that Gemma-2 uses. We found this in a later experiment, after the results above had been seen, so the correction is post hoc. The stored labels are reproduced exactly with that implementation; with eager attention, and with vLLM, the labels agree with each other on all 1{,}161 Llama replies and differ from the stored ones. We relabeled all \cRejudged{} stored replies with the same prompt and parser and kept the rule that both judges must say DONT\_KNOW. The two-judge agreement on Llama rose from $\kappa=\asPrintedKappa$ to \hmLlamaKappa{}, the margin from \asPrintedMargin{} \asPrintedMarginCI{} to \hmLlamaDiff{} \hmLlamaDiffCI, and the unknown-fact gap from \asPrintedUnk{} \asPrintedUnkCI{} to \rcUnk{} \rcUnkCI{} (after the label corrections, from \asPrintedUnkJoint{} \asPrintedUnkJointCI{} to \rcUnkJoint{} \rcUnkJointCI). Agreement between two LLM judges is not validation against people. Gemma-2-9B as a tested model was run with the same implementation; on 300 questions its forced answers were equally correct and its entropies nearly identical ($r=0.999$) with the correct one. Its steering and lens runs were not rerun.

